    "created_at": "2025-02-25T13:32:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-02-25T13:35:02Z",
    "body": "> * **`top-layer-order` CSS property** → Explicitly set top-layer stacking order (similar to `z-index`).\n\nA reasoning against this I often hear is that it would be `z-index` wars all over again. I don’t entirely agree with that take, as with `z-index` you are battling multiple nested levels which you can’t escape, whereas in top layer it’s only space that you are stacking in.\n\nThat said, I personally think having three options for `top-layer-order` would be sufficient: `low`, `normal`, and `high`, in which `high` items – to be used for toast notifications – would always be on top of the `normal` and `low` items. The initial value would be `normal`.\n\nHowever, like you mention, that would not prevent toast notifications being inert when a `<dialog>` is modally opened. That is being discussed in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9936. I believe that having these low/normal/high levels would make that one easier to solve.\n\n_EDIT:_ I hacked something together with your top layer observer and `moveBefore()`: https://codepen.io/bramus/pen/mydEVOR/297c1e8a636d0dc2fb79e4ce7962c117. Authors shouldn’t be expected to do this in order to keep a popover above a dialog interactive.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
